# Windows script files must keep CRLF.
#
# cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only .cmd and .bat files, and the failure is
# misleading: it reports something unrelated, such as a broken interpreter
# path, rather than a line-ending problem. Git would otherwise normalise these
# to LF on checkout and the launcher would stop working for reasons nobody
# could see in the file.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Lich scripts must keep LF, for the opposite reason to the block above.
#
# `.lic` files are Ruby, loaded by the Ruby interpreter rather than cmd.exe,
# so nothing here mis-parses LF - the copy Lich is running right now is pure
# LF, 2,035 lines, zero CR bytes, measured rather than assumed.
#
# Without a rule they inherit whatever `core.autocrlf` each machine happens to
# have, which is `true` on this one. That cost a real half-hour on 27 Aug 2026:
# the same commit checked out as 74,410 bytes on one clone and 76,445 on
# another, and the 2,035-byte gap - exactly one CR per line - was read as a
# stale install and "fixed" by overwriting a current file with itself in CRLF.
#
# Pinning it makes the checkout deterministic, so two machines comparing this
# file are comparing its content rather than each other's git settings.
*.lic text eol=lf
